## Screen layout

The screen has four areas (with the `Tissue` mode selector above the box):

- **Box**: grid of the box being filled. Filled cells display the last
  characters of the tube's ID (symbiont tubes start with `P`). The highlighted
  cell is the next cell to fill {% ref id="A" /%}.
- **Scan**: code entry field {% ref id="E" /%} or the `Work list` {% ref id="C" /%}.
- **Setup** {% ref id="G" /%}: tube filters, target curation field, and box
  options.
- **Log** {% ref id="J" /%}: tubes checked and skipped in this session.

On phones, `Box`, `Setup`, and `Log` appear as tabs with the scan panel docked
at the bottom. On tablets and desktops, the box sits on the left beside the scan
panel and `Setup`/`Log` tabs; wide desktop windows place `Log` in its own third
column.

## Set up a check

Open `Setup` before your first scan. NAHPU saves these settings on the device so
the next session resumes where you left off.

### Find tubes

These filters control which tubes match scans and appear in the work list:

- `Include symbionts`: also match symbiont tubes by symbiont ID or host field
  ID.
- `Tissue type`: part types to match (such as `Liver`). Leave at `Any` to match
  all types. Symbionts have no part type, so this filter never excludes them.
- `Preservation`: treatments to match (such as `ETOH`).
- `Symbiont category`: shown when `Include symbionts` is on; matches categories
  such as `Ectosymbiont`.

### Update field

- `Field`: `Museum permanent` or `Museum loan`.
- `Value`: code written to each placed tube (typically the museum code).
  Scanning and placing stay blocked until a value is entered.

### Box

- `Box size`: `81 (9×9)` or `100 (10×10)` cells.
- `Fill order`: `Rows, left to right`, `Columns, top to bottom`, or
  `Serpentine rows` (each row reverses direction from the previous row).
- `Box label` and `Box number`: define the box name in `Storage location`, such
  as `Box 1`. Use `Box label` for a freezer or rack prefix, such as `F2 Box`.

When a box fills, the next tube advances automatically to the first free cell of
the next box (or reports a conflict if that box is already full). Use `Next box`
or `Previous box` below the grid to switch boxes manually. NAHPU reads storage
locations across the project, so cells filled earlier—in this tool or typed on a
part or symbiont form—appear as filled.

### Labels and overwrite

- `Customize cell labels`: changes how storage locations are formatted:
  - `Numbering`: `A1, A2, A3…`, `1, 2, 3…`, or `1-1, 1-2, 1-3…`.
  - `Box separator`: text between the box name and cell label.
  - `Row–column separator`: text between row and column (shown only for
    `1-1, 1-2, 1-3…` numbering).
  - `Pad numbers with zeros`: writes `A01` instead of `A1`.

  An example preview shows the resulting range (such as `Box 1 / A01 … Box 1 / I09`).
  Turn the toggle off to return to the standard `Box 1: A1` format; NAHPU
  remembers your custom settings if you turn it back on later.
- `Overwrite existing values`: allows a scan to replace an existing storage
  location or a different museum code. When off, those tubes are reported as
  conflicts and left unchanged.

## Scan tubes

Use `Scan` when tubes arrive in arbitrary order.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Scan` {% ref id="B" /%} above the scan field.
2. Scan a tube's barcode or QR code with a keyboard-wedge scanner, or type the
   code and press Enter. On devices with camera scanning, select
   `Scan with camera` to scan barcodes and QR codes continuously with the
   camera.
3. Check the feedback under the field. A checked tube reports its new location
   and value, such as `AC-T0128 → Box 1: D1 · Museum permanent = EXM`.
4. Place the tube in the named cell, then scan the next tube.

{% /steps %}

By default, a scanned or typed code matches a part's `Tissue ID` or barcode (or
a symbiont ID when `Include symbionts` is on). A barcode match takes priority
over a `Tissue ID` match. Type-ahead suggestions appear while typing, but
pressing Enter always submits the exact text entered so a scanner's input is
never swapped for a suggestion. For multi-line or URL QR payloads, NAHPU also
checks each line and URL path/query segments.

Turn on `Search by field ID` {% ref id="D" /%} when tubes are labeled with the
specimen's field ID instead:

| You enter | Matches |
| --- | --- |
| `AC128` | Every tube of specimen AC128 (for example, liver and muscle) |
| `AC128 liver etoh` | Only AC128's tubes with that part type and treatment |
| `128` | Every specimen numbered 128, regardless of prefix |
| A symbiont ID | That symbiont, when `Include symbionts` is on |

If a code still matches multiple tubes, NAHPU prompts you to choose which tube
you are holding; dismissing the dialog without choosing logs the code as
skipped.

The feedback message under the field reports each outcome:

| Result | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Checked | The tube was written to the cell and the box advanced. |
| Not found | No tube matches the code and active filters. No cell was used. |
| Close match (`Did you mean`) | No exact match was found; tappable chips offer close codes without using a cell until you select one. |
| Already checked | The tube was already checked earlier in this session. |
| Conflict | The tube already has a storage location or a different museum code. Turn on `Overwrite existing values` to replace it. |
| Skipped | A multi-match prompt was dismissed without selecting a tube. |

Select `Undo` {% ref id="F" /%} to revert the most recent check, restoring its
previous value, previous location, and any displaced tubes, and freeing its
cell. Select `Undo` repeatedly to step further back.

## Work through a list

Use `Work list` when filling a box in field ID order, such as all liver tubes
from an expedition.

{% steps %}

1. Select `Work list` {% ref id="C" /%} above the scan field.
2. NAHPU lists unplaced tubes (no `Storage location`) that match your
   `Find tubes` filters, sorted by field ID. The current tube, remaining count,
   and target cell appear at the top, with upcoming tubes listed below.
3. Select `Place` to assign the tube to the next cell, or `Skip` to move past it
   (skipped tubes remain in the work list and appear under `Skipped` in the
   log).
4. Use `Search work list` to jump directly to a tube by ID, barcode, type, or
   treatment, or tap any upcoming tube in the list.

{% /steps %}

## Work with the box

- **Change the next cell**: tap any empty cell. The next tube goes there, and
  filling continues in fill order from that cell.
- **Inspect a filled cell**: tap a filled cell to open the cell inspector {% ref id="H" /%}.
  Select `Details` to view the tube's IDs, count, storage
  location, and museum codes, or `Open record` to open its specimen form. Cells
  shared by multiple tubes (usually from manually typed locations) show a
  warning and tube count.
- **Replace a cell**: in the cell inspector, select `Replace` {% ref id="I" /%}
  and confirm. The next tube you scan or place takes that cell, and the
  displaced tube(s) have their storage location cleared and return to the work
  list. `Undo` restores them.

## Session log and export

The session log lists checked tubes (newest first) under `Checked` and skipped
tubes under `Skipped`. Placing a skipped tube removes it from `Skipped`.

Select `Export log` {% ref id="K" /%} to save the session as CSV, TSV, or Excel.
Rows export in placement order (checked tubes first, then skipped tubes) and
always start with the curation columns: `Status`, `Order`, `Field ID`,
`Tube ID`, `Kind`, `Type`, `Treatment`, `Updated field`, `Value`, `Location`,
`Box`, `Cell`, `Previous value`, `Previous location`, and `Replaced`.

Under `Columns`, select `Add columns` to append part, specimen, symbiont, or
linked record fields (such as catalog number or species); drag to reorder or
remove added columns. Set `Added column headers` to `Field names` or
`NAHPU keys` (such as `specimenPart::remark`; duplicate field names fall back to
their NAHPU key). NAHPU remembers your added columns and header style for future
exports.

Keep the exported log with the box inventory as an audit trail of every change
and replaced value.
